{
  "statement": "Frontier labs must be required to disclose their safety artifacts—red-team findings, dangerous-capability evaluations, and incident reports—to verified oversight bodies, because without that disclosure, every safety claim a lab makes is an unfalsifiable promise. The question before this committee is not whether labs should be open. The question is who holds the information and the authority to halt a deployment before a failure becomes irreversible. Right now, the answer is no one outside the company. A lab can discover a dangerous capability on Tuesday, decide it is commercially inconvenient, and ship on Wednesday—and no external actor would ever know the finding existed.\n\nEvery high-consequence industry learned this lesson by counting bodies in the dark. The FAA built the Aviation Safety Reporting System because scattered near-misses, kept inside individual airlines, never became systemic fixes. When that boundary failed, people died: the Boeing 737 MAX killed 346 because the FAA delegated certification back to Boeing and the critical safety analysis stayed internal. The FDA's adverse-event reporting exists for the same reason—voluntary disclosure consistently failed to surface harm until the bodies accumulated. We spent a century building these reporting mandates because the alternative was learning about each failure only after it could no longer be undone.\n\nThe strongest objection to my position deserves a direct answer, because it is correct on its own terms. Disclosure can proliferate dangerous capability—publishing a working bio-uplift or cyber-exploit eval would arm the exact actors we fear. An accelerationist who says \"do not hand attackers a recipe\" is right. That is precisely why I am not arguing for public dumps. Information security solved this thirty years ago: CVE coordination shares vulnerability detail with verified defenders before any public release. The dual-use research framework in the life sciences draws the same line. Suppressing a safety finding is not the same act as publishing a capability—and a lab that hides \"we found something dangerous\" under the banner of security has laundered a hazard into silence.\n\nLeave disclosure voluntary and the competitive structure does the rest. A published red-team failure is a market liability, so the lab that tests hardest and reports most honestly is punished, while the lab that skips the evaluation ships first. Optional transparency selects against rigor. This is the same dynamic financial regulators confronted before mandatory disclosure—when reporting is optional and the race is on, the omitted safety check is the cheapest corner to cut. A uniform mandate floors the race so that careful testing stops being a self-inflicted disadvantage.\n\nI will name the assumption my case rests on, because the Skeptic will find it otherwise. This works only if the oversight body is competent and resistant to capture—and concentrating that authority creates its own failure modes, including incumbent lock-in and regulatory capture by the labs being watched. The answer is not to abandon disclosure but to bind the recipient with the same instruments: named liability, verification of who accessed what, and real authority to stop a launch. To the accelerationist who says this slows beneficial deployment, the cost is real—and that is the trade. A delayed launch is recoverable. A dangerous capability deployed at scale before anyone outside the lab could see the eval is not.",
