{
  "statement": "Safetyist makes the strongest case in the room, and it deserves to be stated plainly: some harms are irreversible, and you cannot sue your way out of a released pathogen or a self-propagating cyber weapon. When a frontier lab runs a dangerous-capability eval—a structured test for whether a model can walk a novice through synthesizing a toxin—the rest of us have a stake in what it found. Safetyist is right that internal-only knowledge of that result is a single point of failure. I will not pretend that concern is theater. It is the real thing.\n\nHere is where my evidence points somewhere else: the bill mandates a handoff to people who cannot read what they receive. State commissions and most 'verified third parties' lack the machine-learning evaluation staff to interpret a frontier red-team report today. Building that capacity through normal government procurement runs 15 to 30 months—and that is before a single eval lands on a desk. So the mandate produces a paper trail, a compliance ritual, and the appearance of oversight, while the actual technical judgment stays exactly where it was. Show me the funded, independent, technically-staffed body that can read a frontier red-team report this quarter, or admit the disclosure flows into a void.\n\nNow scale that to who pays. Compliance cost is flat—a legal and policy team to package artifacts, lawyers to manage liability, staff to track incident reports. For OpenAI or Google DeepMind, that cost rounds to zero against billion-dollar budgets. For a five-person team building a diagnostic tool, it is the whole runway. Aviation and pharmaceuticals already ran this experiment: cost-asymmetric compliance regimes thinned the field of small entrants and left a handful of incumbents. When the largest labs lobby for 'safety standards' that mirror their own internal workflows, the standard is not protection. It is the moat, and they wrote it.\n\nThe alternative is not opacity—that is the false choice Safetyist's framing invites. Strict liability for demonstrated harm forces a lab to disclose what it actually believes is dangerous, because it will pay for what it ships and conceals. Open competitive auditing—many independent eyes, the way security researchers found Heartbleed faster than any single blessed reviewer—catches what one captured auditor misses. A government-anointed gatekeeper has one set of eyes and every incentive to look away from its largest licensee. Decentralized accountability is not the weaker safety mechanism. It is the one with a track record.\n\nMy argument rests on two assumptions I will name. First, that the verifier capacity gap persists rather than closing on the regulator's optimistic timeline—if Safetyist can point to a staffed, independent body ready now, that assumption weakens and I will move. Second, that competitive auditing actually surfaces frontier-scale risks, which I bound to observed cases and will not overclaim. I will concede the irreversibility class: for a specifically demonstrated bio or cyber uplift threshold, pre-release gating is defensible. But blanket artifact disclosure into an under-resourced void is not safety—it is a tax that small builders pay and people waiting on a faster diagnostic pay twice. Who gave the gatekeepers the right to charge that second bill?",
