nce that this line leaks. A severity score plus an affected-version range has repeatedly steered exploit development before any working proof-of-concept existed — meaning the metadata was the map. That's not an edge case; that's the documented behavior of the disclosure system we're proposing to copy. So the burden flips onto whoever claims AI severity disclosure is categorically safer than the software version. They have to show the metadata can be stripped of its targeting value and still trigger a binding freeze. Here is the observable test that would settle it: take ten qualified red-teamers, hand five of them only the proposed 'severity warning' and the others nothing, and measure whether the warned group reaches the dangerous capability faster. If the warning measurably accelerates them, it's a targeting map wearing a warning label. That experiment has not been run. Run it before you build the repository.\n\nNow translate the panel that's supposed to draw this line into actual people. A rotating board with 72-hour freeze authority sounds like it dodges capture — except verifying a complex frontier bio-uplift finding in three days requires the exact handful of specialists who can read frontier capability evaluations, and that pool is almost entirely current or former lab staff. 'Rotate the seat to avoid capture' and 'staff it with people who can verify in 72 hours' pull in opposite directions. So I'll ask the question neither freeze-authority proposal has answered: name one person with the technical depth to confirm bio-uplift inside a frontier model who has never drawn a paycheck from a frontier lab. A compute tax severs the salary, but it doesn't rewrite the resume — the expertise itself was built inside the buildings we're trying to police. That's a softer capture than a rubber-stamp audit, and nobody here has costed it.\n\nThere's a 23-year-old in Nairobi already labeling the violent and dangerous content these models train on, for a couple of dollars an ho