    {
      "argument": "Mandatory pre-deployment disclosure-and-freeze regimes only bind labs operating inside the jurisdiction's enforcement reach, so the binding constraint of the whole architecture is whether the most safety-indifferent capable actor (a foreign state lab, an open-weight fork, or a non-compliant offshore entity) is governed by it at all — making the marginal safety value of domestic disclosure rules a function of capability diffusion and international coordination, not of how cleverly the domestic schema is designed.",
      "why_missing": "The Safetyist frames this as a domestic regulatory-design problem and would resist conceding that a well-built mandate could be near-worthless if the frontier moves offshore, because that undercuts the urgency of the disclosure mandate itself. The Accelerationist gestures at open-weight distribution as a value but won't push the diffusion point hard here because it would imply the capability spreads regardless of any regime — including their own preferred liability/auditor scheme — undermining their constructive proposal. The Skeptic relentlessly follows power and cost but stays inside the institutional-design frame (capture, staffing, the Nairobi labeler) and never zooms out to whether the regulated set even contains the actor that matters.",
