ty is the preparer; the independent confirmation that the elicited number crossed a notarized threshold needs metrological independence, not biographical purity. That is a staffing answer the Safetyist's protocol-verification model still owes — and one their architecture, which leaves the substantive call sealed in the originating community, has not produced.\n\nThis also dissolves the honeypot problem I raised earlier and the capture problem the Skeptic raised, because they point at the same fix. A single cleared room holding the most detailed offensive-capability findings on the planet is the highest-payoff breach target in existence — the 2015 OPM breach took 21.5 million cleared-personnel records including the SF-86 background files, and the Manning and Snowden disclosures moved compartmented material out of access-controlled systems that were supposed to be unbreachable. Clearances and access controls did not hold those repositories. So stop concentrating the decision in the room. Decentralize the freeze onto a notarized metric an outside statistician can audit, and the captured pool loses the one thing capture buys it: an unfalsifiable veto.\n\nThe Skeptic will say I have only moved capture upstream — that the same fifty people design the benchmark and set the threshold, so notarizing it just relocates the rigging to the rubric. That objection has teeth, and it is the real fight. But there is a hard difference between a number you can bend after you see the result and a number you bent before anyone could check your work. Pre-registration does not make the threshold neutral — it makes the rigging visible to the next independent team that runs the same notarized protocol and gets a different answer. Name me the disclosure regime that does better than 'the captured pool must commit its rules in writing before it sees the outcome, and an outside team can rerun them.' If you cannot, then the choice is not between my flawed metric and a clean one — it is between a 