ublished severity score plus an affected-version range has repeatedly been enough to direct exploit development before any proof-of-concept ever dropped — attackers read the contour and work backward. Confirming 'this specific model class gives non-expert bio-uplift at capability X' is not a warning label. It is a targeting map: it names the model, the capability class, and roughly the prompt regime worth probing. The sealed vault doesn't stay sealed once its existence and its outline are public knowledge — you've told every state lab on earth exactly where to dig.\n\nWhich is why the 'smaller attack surface' math is backwards. A single repository of maximally sensitive dual-use findings doesn't shrink the surface — it concentrates the payoff per breach, and payoff is what determines how hard you get targeted. Show me one access-controlled archive of the most sensitive dual-use data on the planet that has survived a decade without a breach, an insider, or a subpoena. I'll wait. The honest framing isn't central-vault versus distributed-silo. It's static-target versus moving-target: a single honeypot is the one thing every adversary aims at for ten years, while compartmented, rotating reproduction detail gives no one a fixed bullseye. Bind disclosure to a demonstrated threshold and an independent trigger — keep the recipe moving.\n\nAnd here's the cost nobody's pricing. Every month spent designing the perfect unbreachable vault is a month the threshold model ships into the world with no trigger wired to anything at all. The choice in front of us is not safe-vault versus leaky-silo — it's a flawed mechanism running this quarter against a flawless mechanism that arrives after the capability is already deployed and irreversible. I'll take the rotating panel with a stop-cord today over the architecturally pure honeypot that's still in committee when the model crosses the line.",
