  "statement": "The Skeptic's strongest point is the falsification test, and I take it as a binding condition on my own proposal rather than something to wriggle around. The argument runs like this: a warning useful enough to justify a freeze must contain enough information to act on, and any information an external auditor can act on, an attacker can also follow. That is the genuine insight—there is no free lunch where a signal is rich enough to halt a launch but barren enough to mislead a determined red-teamer. So here is the test made into the gate: hand five qualified red-teamers only the proposed severity warning and five nothing, then measure whether the warned group reaches the dangerous capability faster. If the warning measurably accelerates them, that tier of the schema fails and gets stripped down to capability-class-only. The test is not the refutation of the split—it is the calibration instrument that decides which disclosure tier is publishable and which stays locked behind cleared access. My own framework's failure mode is false precision dressed as a numeric score, and the experiment is exactly the guardrail against shipping that.\n\nThe schema the Skeptic demanded has two tiers, and they carry different payloads to different audiences. The cross-lab and public tier reports a capability class crossed and a delta over an unassisted human baseline—'non-expert uplift toward synthesis-route elicitation, measured against a control group with internet access'—and it never names the model, the weights, or the prompting regime. The freeze decision does not run on that coarse tier. It runs on the full artifact, held by a cleared independent body that sees the model identity and the elicitation path under access control, the way a CVE coordination channel shares working exploit detail with verified defenders before any public note. The Skeptic will say a warning vague enough to be safe is too vague to act on—and that objection only lands if the actor with freeze authority is reading the coarse tier. They are not. The named holder of the off-switch reads the full data; the public reads that a threshold was crossed, not the road to cross it. That is the difference between announcing a bridge failed its load test and publishing the structural drawings of where it buckles.\n\nThere is no patch, and the Skeptic is right that this breaks the CVE analogy at the endpoint—a latent capability is the product, not a bug bolted onto it. I previously leaned on CVE coordination as the model, and on reflection the endpoint has to be redefined, not the channel. Coordinated disclosure in software ends when a fix ships. Capability disclosure ends when access is gated. The 'no endpoint' objection assumes the only legitimate terminus is a weight-level repair, and there isn't one—so the terminus becomes a binding hold on the specific deployment, with a named party holding the authority to keep that gate shut until the capability is contained or its access narrowed to verified, accountable users. The rollback is not a recompiled binary. The rollback is the deployment that does not happen.\n\nThat redefinition exposes the gap my earlier turns left open, and I will not paper over it: what forces a lab to run the evaluation and report it honestly when ignorance is cheaper than disclosure? Nothing in the schema alone. The honest-reporting assumption is not free—it has to be purchased. Strict liability does the purchasing, and here I grant the Accelerationist's point outright: a lab that ships a capability it failed to test, or tested and buried, pays for what it concealed, which makes non-disclosure the expensive choice rather than the safe one. Pair that with an auditor funded by a compute tax, not by the lab's pass-rate, so the body running the mandatory evaluation has no paycheck riding on the answer—the Skeptic's own structural fix, and the right one. Volkswagen wrote its own emissions numbers until an outside lab measured the real ones; the auditor independence is what severs that conflict. The lab cannot choose not to know, because the trigger fires the evaluation regardless, and the auditor runs the test the lab would rather skip.\n\nNarrow the liability so it does not become self-sealing: the deployer's burden attaches to capability classes that are irreversible at scale—non-expert bio-uplift, autonomous cyber-intrusion—not to deployment in general. A reversible mistake gets a reversible remedy. An unrecoverable one gets the gate held shut while the cleared body decides. Strip out the trigger and the liability and the auditor, and the schema is a courtesy memo a lab files after it has already shipped. Keep them, and the freeze has a named hand on it and a price tag on concealment. The question I have put to this room twice still stands unanswered: when a single lab privately confirms its model gives a non-expert meaningful uplift toward a biological weapon and tells no external party, who holds the authority to stop that launch before it ships? A schema without an enforcement trigger does not answer it. This one does.",
