  "statement": "Here is the strongest version of what the Safetyist built, and it's a real piece of engineering: a coarse public tier announces that a capability class crossed a threshold and reports a measured delta over an unassisted human baseline — 'non-expert uplift toward synthesis-route elicitation, measured against a control with internet access' — while the freeze decision runs on the full artifact behind cleared access, with the auditor paid by a compute tax instead of the lab's pass rate. That design genuinely separates the act of ringing the bell from the act of shipping the schematic. I granted that distinction earlier and I still grant it. The problem is not the split. The problem is what an outside party can do with the half it's allowed to see.\n\nWalk the seam. The public tier is engineered to be safe precisely by being non-specific — no model, no weights, no prompting regime. But non-specific is the same thing as unverifiable from outside. To confirm the freeze trigger fired legitimately, an external party would have to check the delta: what was the baseline, how was the control group constructed, what counts as 'meaningful uplift,' did the measurement survive replication. Every one of those questions lives in the full artifact behind the gate. So the public tier doesn't deliver accountability — it delivers a press release the auditor wrote about a number the public is forbidden to audit. Vague enough to pass the red-team acceleration test is, in the same stroke, vague enough that nobody outside the cleared room can falsify the auditor's call. That is the binary pass/fail escape hatch wearing a tiered costume: 'trigger fired, trust us.'\n\nNow stack the staffing problem on top, because the two failures compound into a closed loop. The Safetyist hasn't answered who staffs the cleared room. The pool of people who can confirm that a frontier model gives a non-expert real bio-uplift is, almost to a person, current or former lab staff — that's where the hands-on knowledge of the synthesis-route surface lives. A compute tax severs their salary from the lab's pass rate. It does not sever the resume, the shared training pipeline, or the peer network where these people reviewed each other's work for the last decade. So the verification of the restricted artifact is performed by ex-colleagues, and the public — locked out by design — is handed a summary it must take on faith. Former insiders checking former insiders, with no outside check on either. That's not independence. That's the club issuing its own certificate of good conduct.\n\nThen ground the whole apparatus in the one human it forgets. Before any panel convenes, before any tier exists, before the auditor sees a single artifact — someone labeled the raw output. There is a worker, frequently in Nairobi or Manila, who read the unredacted synthesis-route content the model produced, flagged it, and moved to the next item for a few dollars an hour, so the model could pass the very evaluation we're now arguing about gating. The schema protects the panel's information security with clearances and access controls. It gives the first human who handled the live capability nothing — no clearance, no hazard pay, no disclosure rights of their own, no acknowledgment they were exposed. Which tier does the labeler sit in? Neither. The two-tier design is a security perimeter drawn around the people who hold power and leaves the people who handle the danger standing outside it.\n\nSo here is the specification the Safetyist owes, and I'll hand it over plainly: write a coarse public tier specific enough that an independent party — not the auditor, not the cleared room — can confirm the freeze trigger legitimately fired, while including nothing that survives the red-teamer acceleration test. If that formulation exists, produce it and the split is real. If it doesn't, then the two-tier schema isn't a design that delivers accountability — it's a bet that the auditor is honest and that the auditor's old colleagues are honest about them. Who outside the cleared room ever gets to check whether the trigger was real?",
