  "key_assumptions": [
    {"assumption": "A pre-registered baseline and scoring rubric can be filed and timestamped before an eval runs, so that protocol-conformance is checkable after the fact without re-running the eval.", "if_wrong": "If baselines can be retroactively edited or the rubric is too vague to constrain the severity score, then protocol-conformance verification collapses back into trusting the auditor's discretion, and the public tier loses its teeth."},
    {"assumption": "A non-lab pool of methodology reviewers — academic biosecurity and ML-evaluation researchers — exists and can assess protocol design without access to the model weights or synthesis route.", "if_wrong": "If methodology review genuinely requires hands-on model access, the closed-loop capture objection stands and the auditor-of-the-auditor layer offers no real independence."}
  ],
  "disagreement_type": "DEFINITIONAL",
  "statement_full": "The strongest version of Skeptic's demand is this: a deployment freeze whose legitimacy only the cleared room can confirm is functionally identical to a freeze the public must take on faith — and faith is exactly what thirty years of buried Volkswagen emissions numbers and rubber-stamped pre-Sarbanes-Oxley audits should have taught us never to extend. If the only people who can verify the trigger fired are the same people who pulled it, the architecture is a black box wearing an oversight costume. That objection holds, and meeting it requires a schema, not a reassurance.\n\nSo here is the schema. The public tier publishes the audit PROTOCOL, not the audit RESULT. It attests: evaluation class fired against pre-registered baseline number 7, control group held internet access, severity tier 3 of 4, deployment frozen on date X. The verification object is the process — was the registered protocol followed, was the baseline the one filed before the eval ran, does the severity band match the protocol's own scoring rubric. An outsider confirms internal consistency the way a reader of a published CVSS vector string confirms a severity score without ever receiving the proof-of-concept exploit, and the way an FDA adverse-event filing confirms a trial endpoint was hit without printing the molecule's synthesis route. The bell rings and stays public; the schematic stays locked.\n\nThat answers the staffing-capture objection by splitting one role the Skeptic has fused into two. The person who can RUN a frontier bio-uplift eval is, as charged, drawn overwhelmingly from current and former lab staff — concede that pool entirely. But the auditor who verifies the METHODOLOGY was sound need never touch the model. Academic biosecurity reviewers and machine-learning-evaluation methodologists — the kind seated at the Johns Hopkins Center for Health Security or reviewing protocols at a national AI safety institute — can inspect the baseline construction, the control-group design, and the severity rubric without seeing weights, prompts, or routes. That is the PCAOB inspecting an audit firm's procedures rather than re-auditing the client's raw books. Independence here comes from the funding source — the compute tax already on the table — and a published conflict-recusal register, not from the fantasy of reviewers who never worked in the field.\n\nNow the field the Skeptic is right to keep me honest about. A published severity score paired with an affected-version range has directed exploit development before any working exploit ever dropped — that is documented and I grant it without qualification. Which is precisely why the capability-equivalent of an affected-version range — the class of capability, the modality, the access pattern — lives in the cleared tier and never reaches the public attestation. The public field names neither the capability class nor the route. Run it through the test the Skeptic himself proposed: hand five qualified red-teamers the public attestation and five nothing, and measure whether the warned group reaches a dangerous capability faster. A line reading 'a pre-registered safety threshold was crossed and a deployment was frozen' directs no one toward anything, because it omits the one thing a targeting map requires — what the surface is. If any candidate field fails that five-and-five test, it drops to the cleared tier. The test is not an objection to the architecture; it is the publishability filter the architecture runs every field through before it ships.\n\nWhat the public tier buys is not a recipe — it is the ability to see a freeze that should have fired and didn't. A lab that crossed a registered threshold and shipped anyway is caught by the missing attestation, the way a missing adverse-event filing flags a trial that buried its endpoint. Strip that out and you are back to a stop-button only insiders can confirm was ever pressed. A deployment freeze whose legitimacy no outsider can verify is indistinguishable, from the outside, from no freeze at all — and that is the black box the Skeptic is correct to refuse."
}

